  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nNested tags can become very unclear.  Allowing matching class names would make it easier to author html documents.  I am looking at documents with 8 nested div tags right now!  How am I supposed to see which one was just ended?\n\n```\n<div class=\"row\">\n   <div class=\"col\">\n      <div class=\"mine\">\n         aha\n       </div class=\"mine\">\n    </div class=\"col\">\n </div class=\"row\">\n```\n\nthis should be optional. in many situations, one prefers not having to restate the opening class.\n\nit can be ignored by browsers.  it can be checked by a lint type utility. \n\nit is the same reason why we don't have `</end>` as the ending tag for each and every opening tag.   when long ago we didn't have documents with so many nested divs and spans, i.e., in very early versions of HTML, the extended readability of using the name again was indeed taken care of.  but nowadays, we have lost the clearer open-end tag correspondence in many cases.\n\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\n```\n<div class=\"row\">\n   <div class=\"col\">\n      <div class=\"mine\">\n         aha\n       </div> <!-- class=\"mine\" -->\n    </div> <!--  class=\"col\" -->\n </div> <!-- class=\"row\" -->\n```\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\n\nsee above.\n\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
